{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and ambitious ruler known for his military prowess and political maneuvering. Born around 1134 he inherited the County at a young age upon the death of his father Raymond II. His reign was marked by constant warfare and diplomacy navigating the complex political landscape of the Levant.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond III's early life was steeped in the conflicts of the Crusader states. He participated in military campaigns from a young age demonstrating his martial abilities. His father Raymond II had established a strong albeit precarious position for the County of Tripoli a vital link between Europe and the East. Raymond III's ascension to the County was not without its challenges facing internal rebellions and external threats from Muslim forces. He quickly consolidated his power suppressing dissent and strengthening the county's defenses. He also focused on developing Tripoli's economy fostering trade and attracting merchants.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw him engage in numerous conflicts with Saladin the Ayyubid sultan who posed a constant threat to the Crusader states. These conflicts included sieges battles and skirmishes testing the strength of Tripoli's fortifications and the loyalty of its people. He formed alliances with other Crusader rulers seeking to counter Saladin's growing power. He actively participated in the Third Crusade although his involvement was relatively limited compared to some other nobles.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at forging alliances and navigating treacherous political waters. He maintained relations with the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II a powerful figure in Europe seeking support for his campaigns against Saladin. He also cultivated relationships with other Crusader lords such as Baldwin of Jerusalem and Bohemond of Antioch. These alliances were crucial for maintaining Tripoli's security and projecting its influence in the region. His diplomatic efforts extended beyond military alliances encompassing trade agreements and cultural exchanges.\n\nHe invested in the infrastructure of Tripoli including strengthening its walls and fortresses. He also supported the construction of churches and mosques reflecting his policy of religious tolerance. This policy helped to maintain stability within the county and attract both Christian and Muslim residents.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 after a long and eventful reign. He left behind a County of Tripoli that was relatively stable and prosperous despite the constant threats from Muslim forces. He was remembered as a capable military leader a shrewd diplomat and a dedicated ruler. His reign solidified Tripoli's position as a key player in the Crusader states. His policies of religious tolerance and economic development laid the foundation for the county's continued prosperity for decades to come. He is considered one of the most important rulers of Tripoli during the Crusader period.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.583032467868179e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.2649508537142538e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob"